-- S-instantiated-reselect (#1909), THE ISSUE'S OWN FILED REPRO, and the
-- OVER-NARROWING guard its two-ground-impl siblings cannot be.
--
-- Hand-derived from DICT-SEMANTICS §3 BEFORE running anything.  A general
-- `impl Tag (Wrap a)` sits beside a specific `impl Tag (Wrap Bool)`, so each
-- call's matching set has TWO members and §3 takes the min⊑: `nest True` poses
-- `Tag (Wrap Bool)` and must select the SPECIFIC impl, `nest 1` poses
-- `Tag (Wrap Int)`, which the specific impl does not match at all, and must
-- select the GENERAL one.  `specificW/genericW`.
--
-- 🚨 THIS IS THE ROW THAT FAILS IF THE FIX OVER-NARROWS.  Its siblings assert
-- that a per-instantiation goal is REACHED; this one additionally asserts that
-- reaching it does not lose the general instance -- a fix that routed every
-- structured predicate straight to a ground impl would print `specificW/` and
-- then fail to select anything for `nest 1`.  MEASURED at the pre-slice base
-- `bffced42` on this tree: `genericW/genericW` at exit 0 on check, run and build
-- -- i.e. base got the FIRST call wrong (the abstraction-time pick against the
-- rigid goal `Wrap a` is the general impl, and both instantiations inherited it)
-- and the second right by accident.
--
-- ⚠️ ONE CALL IS NOT ENOUGH, which is why this file does not stop at `nest True`.
-- #1909's filed repro is the single call, and a single `genericW -> specificW`
-- move is equally consistent with "now always picks the most specific impl in the
-- program", which is a different and wrong rule.  The `nest 1` arm is what
-- discriminates the two.
--
-- ⚠️ TWO `impl Tag` BLOCKS, so Section 4 derives a permutation pair.  DICT §3
-- forbids declaration order deciding a winner even when both candidates match.
data Wrap a = Wrap a

interface Tag t where
  tagOf : t -> String

impl Tag (Wrap a) where
  tagOf _ = "genericW"

impl Tag (Wrap Bool) where
  tagOf _ = "specificW"

nest : Tag (Wrap a) => a -> String
nest x = tagOf (Wrap x)

main = println "\{nest True}/\{nest 1}"
